   $q$The flood is never narrated directly; readers learn of it only through eleven blank rows and one short remark. What does this choice contribute to the story?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It hides the flood's details so that readers will doubt whether the flood took place at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows that the author considers the flood less important than the daily temperature readings.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"It makes readers experience the gap as Priya does, which explains her decision to record what the numbers leave out.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It lets the author describe the rescue at length from the point of view of the Delacroix boy.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It makes readers experience the gap as Priya does, which explains her decision to record what the numbers leave out.$q$, $q$Filtering the flood through the ledger puts readers in Priya's position, facing "missing data" that hides a human event, which motivates her final act of typing column seven's story. The flood is confirmed in Thorne's remark, so readers have no reason to doubt it.$q$, 'draft', 'c160e63c-3499-53c0-b8dc-ad7ea859f370')
