-- Passage rg12-character-setting-plot-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('abf12121-e77a-5ab8-8b6e-30491211e8f8', 'rg12-character-setting-plot-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Dryer Clock$q$, $q$The chess club on Corliss Avenue had no clubhouse, no trophies, and no clocks. It met every Saturday morning in Mr. Baptiste's laundromat, at two folding tables wedged between the change machine and the long row of dryers, and it used the dryers to keep time. You started your game when you started your load. When the buzzer sounded, the game was over, and Mr. Baptiste, who was strict about it and who did not play, declared whoever was ahead on the board the winner.

Wen Li had joined in October, after her school's team folded. She was used to tournament halls, silent rooms where every move was punched into a digital clock. The laundromat was loud. Machines thumped and sloshed. A toddler ran laps around the folding tables. Wen lost her first four games, not because her opponents were stronger but because she kept planning long attacks that the buzzer cut off halfway.

Her fifth opponent was Mrs. Odunsi, who had driven the number nine bus for thirty-one years and who loaded her dryer with exactly one quilt. Wen had learned by now that one quilt meant forty minutes, longer than anyone else's cycle. She set up a slow, careful attack on the king's side.

Twenty minutes in, Mrs. Odunsi stood, opened her dryer, and pulled out the quilt, still damp.

"It's fine," she said, sitting back down. "I'll hang it at home."

The buzzer would not come now. Wen looked at the board and realized she had no idea how long she had. Every plan she had prepared depended on knowing when the game would end. She stopped trying to finish the attack and began, for the first time that fall, to simply play the position in front of her.

She won in eleven moves. When she looked up, Mrs. Odunsi was folding the damp quilt, smiling, as if that had been the point.$q$, 'draft')
