  ('477ec402-e303-56b8-9f5a-c2b7fd231777', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Orsolya is introduced as refusing payment and saying the river "should be known as it truly was." How does this introduction set up her later conflict?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Her words reveal that she secretly knew the river would move and drew the map wrong on purpose.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Her generosity makes the villagers afraid to complain, so the problem goes unmentioned for ten years.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Her devotion to truth hardens into loyalty to her own record, so the same value both causes the conflict and drives her change.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Her refusal of payment shows she is careless about her work, which is why the map is wrong from the start.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Her devotion to truth hardens into loyalty to her own record, so the same value both causes the conflict and drives her change.$q$, $q$Wanting the river known "as it truly was" leads her to insist "It is the river that is wrong," yet that value also explains why she finally dates and redraws her maps. The map was exact when made, so she was not careless, and the villagers do complain.$q$, 'draft', 'a0b3406e-1f54-5269-ae36-e6c1b5421933')
